<p><strong>No, not all Japanese Wagyu is A5.</strong> The confusion is understandable because A5 dominates international marketing and premium product listings. Wagyu and A5, however, describe different things: Wagyu relates to recognised Japanese cattle breeds, while A5 is a grade awarded to a carcass after evaluation.</p>
<p>Wagyu can be A3, A4, A5 or carry other letter-number combinations. Understanding this prevents lower grades from being mistaken for fake or poor-quality Wagyu.</p>
<h2>Wagyu is the category; A5 is the grade</h2>
<p>Japanese Black, Brown, Shorthorn and Polled are the principal recognised Wagyu breeds. A carcass does not stop being Wagyu because it receives A4 or A3.</p>
<p>The letter A, B or C describes yield; the number 1–5 describes quality. A3 therefore means yield A and quality 3, A4 means yield A and quality 4, and A5 means yield A and quality 5.</p>
<h2>What changes between A3, A4 and A5?</h2>
<p>Quality considers marbling, meat colour and brightness, firmness and texture, and fat colour and quality. The lowest criterion constrains the final quality grade.</p>
<p>For marbling, BMS 3–4 corresponds to quality grade 3, BMS 5–7 to grade 4 and BMS 8–12 to grade 5.</p>
<h2>What is A3 like?</h2>
<p>A3 can show clear marbling without approaching extreme A5 density. For diners used to premium European beef, it may offer a useful bridge between a meat-forward steak and very rich Japanese Wagyu.</p>
<h2>What does A4 offer?</h2>
<p>A4 often provides an attractive balance. BMS 5–7 can produce substantial intramuscular fat without necessarily reaching the intensity of BMS 10–12. Diners who want a larger portion or more muscle flavour may prefer it.</p>
<h2>What makes A5 different?</h2>
<p>A5 combines yield A and quality 5. Its marbling criterion covers BMS 8 through 12, so A5 itself includes a range of intensities.</p>
<h2>Does A5 always mean BMS 12?</h2>
<p>No. A5 can carry BMS 8, 9, 10, 11 or 12, provided all other quality criteria also reach grade 5.</p>
<h2>Does A5 always taste better than A4?</h2>
<p>“Better” depends on preference. Official grading is objective, but enjoyment is personal. Some diners love extreme richness; others prefer more obvious muscle character. See our <a href="/en/wagyu-a4-vs-a5-differences/">A4 vs A5 comparison</a>.</p>
<h2>Can A3 be authentic Japanese Wagyu?</h2>
<p>Yes. Authenticity depends on breed, origin and traceability, not on reaching the maximum quality grade.</p>
<h2>Why do export markets show so much A5?</h2>
<p>Gourmet export markets naturally highlight spectacular, high-value products. This can make international shoppers see far more A5 than they would expect across the entire Japanese beef market.</p>
<h2>Which grade is best for a first tasting?</h2>
<p>A5 is logical if you want to experience extreme marbling. A4 may suit somebody who wants more balance. Cut and portion size matter just as much.</p>
<h2>Grade does not replace cut</h2>
<p>Ribeye, striploin and tenderloin from the same A5 carcass do not behave identically. Anatomy still controls texture, fat distribution and cooking.</p>
<h2>Grade does not replace origin</h2>
<p>A5 is not a regional designation. Kobe, Miyazaki, Kagoshima and other brands apply separate origin and production requirements.</p>
<h2>How to compare A3, A4 and A5 offers</h2>
<p>Check origin and traceability first, then grade and BMS, followed by cut, weight and format. Consider the serving size you actually need rather than buying the highest number automatically.</p>
<h2>The conclusion</h2>
<p><strong>Japanese Wagyu does not equal A5.</strong> A3, A4 and A5 are grading results. A5 is the maximum quality grade within yield A, but lower grades remain genuine Wagyu and may suit particular tastes better.</p>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3>Can Wagyu be A3?</h3><p>Yes. Wagyu describes breed identity; A3 is one possible grade.</p>
<h3>Does A4 have less marbling than A5?</h3><p>In BMS terms, grade 4 uses 5–7 while grade 5 uses 8–12.</p>
<h3>Is A5 always BMS 12?</h3><p>No. A5 can fall anywhere from BMS 8 to 12.</p>
<h3>Is A5 always the best purchase?</h3><p>No. Preference, cut, portion, occasion and budget all matter.</p>
